{0 Requests and responses}

Use {!Workero.Request} to read an incoming request and {!Workero.Response} to
answer it. Match on {!Workero.Request.method_} and the path to select a route:

{@ocaml[
let handler req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let url = Request.url req in
  match (Request.method_ req, URL.path url) with
  | GET, "/hello" ->
      let name = Option.value (URL.query url "name") ~default:"world" in
      Response.text (Printf.sprintf "hello, %s\n" name)
  | POST, "/echo" -> Response.text (Request.text req)
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:`Not_Found "not found\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{1 Read the request body}

{!Workero.Request.text} reads the whole body as UTF-8 text.
{!Workero.Request.bytes} reads raw bytes into a string. Both consume the body.
To read a body in chunks, get its stream with {!Workero.Request.body} and read
the stream. Getting the stream does not itself consume it. A request without a
body returns [None]. See {{!page-streams}Streams} for stream readers and
{{!page-forms}Forms and file uploads} for form bodies.

A body can be consumed only once. Call {!Workero.Request.clone} before reading
if you need to read it twice.

{1 Work with headers}

Header names are case-insensitive. {!Workero.Request.header} and
{!Workero.Response.header} read a joined value. For an ordinary repeated header,
{!Workero.Request.header_all} and {!Workero.Response.header_all} return a list
with one joined value, not one item per original header. For [set-cookie],
[header_all] returns one item per cookie. Do not split a [set-cookie] value at
commas: a cookie can contain a comma.

{1 Send a response}

{!Workero.Response.text} sends text, {!Workero.Response.html} sends HTML, and
{!Workero.Response.make} sends raw bytes. Pass [~status] and [~headers] to set
the status and headers. For a response without a body, use
[Response.empty `No_Content]. To send a redirect, use
{!Workero.Response.redirect}. Named statuses that do not allow a body do not
type-check with the body constructors. A numeric [`Status n] still type-checks:
an invalid status or a non-empty body with status 204, 205, or 304 raises
[Invalid_argument] at runtime. See {{!page-streams}Streams} for a streamed
response and {!Workero.Response} for the status rules.

To forward a fetched response with one added header, keep its existing headers
when you call {!Workero.Response.copy}:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let upstream = fetch "https://example.com/" in
  let headers = Response.headers upstream @ [ ("x-via", "workero") ] in
  Response.copy ~headers upstream

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

[Response.copy ~headers] replaces the entire header list. Without [~headers],
it keeps the existing headers. Both responses share one body stream, which
can be consumed only once. Use {!Workero.Response.clone} before reading if
you need two copies. See {{!page-fetch}Fetch} for outgoing requests.

{1 Reference}

See {!Workero.Method}, {!Workero.Request}, {!Workero.Response},
{!Workero.Status}, and {!Workero.Cf} for the complete API. For URL parsing
and query parameters, see {{!page-url}URLs}.
